       POSIX.1‐2001, SVr4, 4.4BSD (first appeared in 4.2BSD).

NNOOTTEESS
       For stream sockets, once a ccoonnnneecctt(2) has been performed, either socket
       can call ggeettppeeeerrnnaammee() to obtain the address of the  peer  socket.   On
       the  other  hand,  datagram  sockets  are connectionless.  Calling ccoonn‐‐
       nneecctt(2) on a datagram socket merely sets the peer address for  outgoing
       datagrams  sent with wwrriittee(2) or rreeccvv(2).  The caller of ccoonnnneecctt(2) can
       use ggeettppeeeerrnnaammee() to obtain the peer address that it  earlier  set  for
       the  socket.   However, the peer socket is unaware of this information,
       and calling ggeettppeeeerrnnaammee() on the peer socket will return no useful  in‐
       formation  (unless  a  ccoonnnneecctt(2)  call was also executed on the peer).
       Note also that the receiver of a datagram can obtain the address of the
       sender when using rreeccvvffrroomm(2).

